report=W/'QIES112A_QUORTEX_CRASH_DIAGNOSTIC.md';text=report.read_text();text+='''\n## Final diagnostic result\n\n**PASS — preserved QIES111 synthetic configuration.** Three fresh80-layer/two-token trials completed:960 successful native subprocesses,12,480 captures matching the original SHA256 map, no recorded signal exits, no safety violations and no KV-integrity mismatch. These are three repeated deterministic workloads, not evidence of every possible input or long-lived process state.\n\n**PASS — bounded stress and recovery cases.** All78 original injected exception hooks and5 invalid-state/dimension refusals returned the expected failure1 while preserving authoritative input. Four selected layer0 cases passed in the preserved ASan/UBSan executable, without sanitizer/leak diagnostics in the instrumented scope. The80-layer repetitions themselves were ordinary builds; no full-stack/full-library sanitizer qualification is claimed. Deliberate termination left no published partial capture or live descendants; retry from the same checkpoint matched all26 independent reference captures. The5-layer seeded sliding/global boundary matched260 captures.\n\nThe four-position extension reused trial3's verified positions0/1, then evaluated positions2/3 through all80 layers in both branches:4,160 additional exact captures,8,320 across the four positions. It is not an independent fourth fresh trial or QIES112 autoregressive language generation. All layer checkpoint identities, positions, checksums and candidate/reference projection-weight digests passed final audit.\n\n**INCONCLUSIVE — historical inference crash.** No matching inference executable/failure record was recovered. The separately recorded PandaCode/WebKit SIGBUS dumps establish desktop failures on November6, not a QUORTEX root cause. That journal's coredump-service memory peaks belong to crash processing, not to the inference workload. The tested ordinary executables, source, three GGML libraries, synthetic input files and guards retain their before-test hashes; frozen refs and disabled legacy libllama permissions are unchanged. No program fix was applied.\n\nFinal machine-readable evidence: `qies112a-diagnostic/final-results.json`, `final-integrity.json`, `system-evidence-after.json`. Per-process raw stdout/stderr, commands, monitor samples and completion records remain under the active series in `active.json`. Accessible kernel/core queries after testing are retained separately; their absence cannot establish what happened in an unlogged historical event.\n\nRecommended next action: preserve the working QIES111 artifact and obtain the exact historical failing command/time/executable hash before any engine change. A separately reviewed **logging-only** supervision improvement should record parent cancellation reason, child exit/signal, last completed layer, stderr and OOM deltas even when the supervising session is interrupted. This closes an attribution gap; it is not a claim that supervision caused the historical crash. Do not relax resource gates or replace the verified engine based on the current evidence.\n'''
report.write_text(text)
